---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const withBase = (p = '') => (base.endsWith('/') ? base : base + '/') + p;
---

<Guide
  title="What the Bhagavad Gita says about anxiety"
  description="Anxiety in the Bhagavad Gita is the mind gripped by an outcome it can't control. Here are the key verses — 2.47, 2.56, 6.35 and more — and how to read them."
  slug="bhagavad-gita-on-anxiety"
  datePublished="2026-09-17"
>
  <p>
    The Bhagavad Gita opens with an anxious man. Before any teaching begins, the warrior Arjuna
    stands between two armies, overwhelmed — his hands trembling, his mind refusing to settle,
    unable to see a way forward. If you have ever frozen at the edge of a hard decision, or lain
    awake turning an outcome over and over, you already know the state the Gita sets out to address.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Gita's view of anxiety is precise: it is the mind clinging to a result it cannot control.
    The cure it offers is not to care less, but to act fully and release your grip on the outcome.
    Here are the verses worth sitting with.
  </p>

  <h2>Act, and let go of the result</h2>
  <p>
    The Gita's most famous line is, at heart, an answer to anxiety. You control your effort, never
    its fruit — so give yourself completely to the doing and set down the rest.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"Your right is to action alone, never to its fruits. Let not the fruit of action be your motive, nor let your attachment be to inaction."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/2/47')}>Bhagavad Gita 2.47</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    So much anxiety lives in the gap between effort and outcome — the replaying, the what-ifs. This
    verse doesn't ask you to stop trying. It asks you to pour yourself into what is actually yours to
    do, and to stop paying rent on the part that isn't.
  </p>

  <h2>Steadiness that adversity can't shake</h2>
  <p>
    A little later, Krishna describes the <em>sthitaprajna</em> — the person of steady wisdom. It is
    a portrait of a mind at rest inside a difficult world.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"One whose mind is untroubled in sorrow and free from craving amid pleasures, who is free from attachment, fear and anger — such a one is called a sage of steady wisdom."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/2/56')}>Bhagavad Gita 2.56</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Notice fear sits right beside craving and anger. The Gita treats them as one family: all are the
    mind pulled out of the present by something it wants or dreads. Steadiness isn't numbness — it's
    the quiet that remains when you stop being yanked around by outcomes.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the mind won't settle — and what helps</h2>
  <p>
    When Krishna prescribes meditation, Arjuna objects that the mind is as impossible to hold as the
    wind. Krishna doesn't disagree — he simply names the two things that gradually tame it.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"The mind is restless and hard to hold, but it can be mastered by practice and by non-attachment."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/6/35')}>Bhagavad Gita 6.35</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    <strong>Practice</strong> (<em>abhyasa</em>) is returning to steadiness again and again, gently,
    without scolding yourself for wandering. <strong>Non-attachment</strong> (<em>vairagya</em>) is
    loosening the grip on how things must turn out. Anxiety rarely yields to a single insight; it
    yields to this quiet, repeated practice.
  </p>

  <h2>How worry becomes something worse</h2>
  <p>
    The Gita also maps how a small anxious craving can spiral. It's worth knowing the chain so you
    can catch it early.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"Dwelling on objects of the senses breeds attachment; from attachment comes desire, and from desire, anger…" — leading, unchecked, to confusion and loss of judgment.</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/2/62')}>Bhagavad Gita 2.62–63</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    The practical takeaway: notice the fixation early, while it's still just a thought circling a
    wanted outcome, before it hardens into frustration and clouds your thinking.
  </p>

  <h2>A place to set it down</h2>
  <p>
    The Gita's final counsel, after eighteen chapters, is essentially: stop carrying all of it alone.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"Abandon all other supports and take refuge in me alone. I will free you from all that binds you; do not grieve."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/18/66')}>Bhagavad Gita 18.66</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Whether you read that devotionally or simply as permission to release the weight you've been
    gripping, the message lands the same way — you were never meant to hold the whole outcome in your
    two hands.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to read next</h2>
  <p>
    Start with <a href={withBase('gita/2')}>Chapter 2</a>, which the tradition calls the Gita in
    miniature, and <a href={withBase('gita/6')}>Chapter 6</a> on meditation and steadying the mind.
    Or, when a particular feeling is with you, open Gita Daily and let a verse meet you there.
  </p>
</Guide>
